          <p>For more information about the support scope of Red Hat Technology Preview features, see <a href="https://access.redhat.com/support/offerings/techpreview/">Technology Preview Features Support Scope</a>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h2 id="installing-pfsr-cli_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the PF Status Relay Operator using the CLI<a href="#installing-pfsr-cli_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Install the PF Status Relay Operator to enable OpenShift Container Platform to use Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) as an active health check on physical functions.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You configured LACP on your upstream switch.</li>
      <li>You configured pod-level bonding for your SR-IOV networks.</li>
      <li>You installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You have cluster-admin privileges.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>openshift-pf-status-relay-operator</code> namespace by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cat << EOF| oc create -f -\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  name: openshift-pf-status-relay-operator\n  annotations:\n    workload.openshift.io/allowed: management\nEOF"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create an <code>OperatorGroup</code> custom resource (CR) by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cat << EOF| oc create -f -\napiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1\nkind: OperatorGroup\nmetadata:\n  name: pf-status-relay-operators\n  namespace: openshift-pf-status-relay-operator\nspec:\n  targetNamespaces:\n  - openshift-pf-status-relay-operator\nEOF"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Subscription</code> CR for the  PF Status Relay Operator by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cat << EOF| oc create -f -\napiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\nkind: Subscription\nmetadata:\n  name: pf-status-relay-operator-subscription\n  namespace: openshift-pf-status-relay-operator\nspec:\n  channel: stable\n  name: pf-status-relay-operator\n  source: redhat-operators\n  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace\nEOF"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To verify that the Operator is installed, enter the following command and then check that output shows <code>Succeeded</code> for the Operator:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csv -n openshift-pf-status-relay-operator -o custom-columns=Name:.metadata.name,Phase:.status.phase"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="installing-pfsr-console_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the PF Status Relay Operator using the web console<a href="#installing-pfsr-console_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Install the PF Status Relay Operator to enable OpenShift Container Platform to use Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) as an active health check on physical functions.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You configured LACP on your upstream switch.</li>
      <li>You configured pod-level bonding for your SR-IOV networks.</li>
      <li>You have cluster-admin privileges.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Install the PF Status Relay Operator:
      <ol>
      <li>In the OpenShift Container Platform web console, click <strong>Ecosystem</strong> → <strong>Software Catalog</strong>.</li>
      <li>Select <strong>PF Status Relay Operator</strong> from the list of available Operators, and then click <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
      <li>On the <strong>Install Operator</strong> page, under <strong>Installed Namespace</strong>, select <strong>Operator recommended Namespace</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul><li>Verify that the PF Status Relay Operator shows the <strong>Status</strong> as <strong>Succeeded</strong> on the Installed Operators dashboard.</li></ul>
      <h2 id="configuring-lacp-sriov_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring the PF Status Relay Operator for LACP state monitoring on SR-IOV networks<a href="#configuring-lacp-sriov_sriov-lacp-sriov" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Use the PF Status Relay Operator to enable Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP) state monitoring for workloads by using pod-level bonding with SR-IOV networks. The Operator monitors the LACP state on physical functions (PF) and changes the link state for attached virtual functions (VF) when it detects an upstream failure. With this approach, you can detect failures on VFs attached to a PF to ensure a timely failover to a backup network path, ensuring high availability for your workloads.</p>
      <p>The following scenario demonstrates how to configure and verify LACP state monitoring for SR-IOV networks:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Create host-level NIC bonds on worker nodes and configure LACP.</li>
      <li>Define SR-IOV network policies to create virtual functions (VFs) on the bonded interfaces.</li>
      <li>Deploy the PF Status Relay Operator to monitor physical functions and the LACP state.</li>
      <li>Verify that pods using these VFs automatically fail over to a backup network path in case of upstream switch failure.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The following scenario demonstrates how to configure and verify LACP state monitoring for SR-IOV networks. This scenario uses SR-IOV network cards with two ports on each node, <code>worker-0</code> and <code>worker-1</code>, with both ports connected to a shared switch to support LACP bonding.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>Nodes must have a NIC that supports SR-IOV.</li>
      <li>The SR-IOV Network Operator is installed.</li>
      <li>The PF Status Relay Operator is installed.</li>
      <li>The physical switch ports connected to the worker nodes are configured for LACP with a fast polling rate.</li>
      <li>The <code>linkState</code> is set to <code>auto</code> or <code>disable</code> for the SR-IOV VFs that you want to monitor. The Operator ignores VFs with the <code>linkState</code> set to <code>enable</code>. The default value for SR-IOV VFs is <code>linkState: auto</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the project namespace by creating a <code>namespace.yaml</code> file such as the following example:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    kubernetes.io/metadata.name: sriov-operator-tests\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: privileged\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: privileged\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: privileged\n    security.openshift.io/scc.podSecurityLabelSync: \"false\"\n  name: <namespace>"} lang="yaml" title="Example namespace.yaml file" />
      <ul><li><code>&lt;namespace&gt;</code> specifies the namespace where you deploy the high-availability pod.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the namespace by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f namespace.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Configure host-level LACP bonds:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy</code> resource for the <code>ens5f0</code> interface on the <code>worker-0</code> node:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1\nkind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: example-bond-f0\nspec:\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: <node_name>\n  desiredState:\n    interfaces:\n      - name: example-bond-f0\n        description: example-bond-f0\n        type: bond\n        state: up\n        mtu: 9216\n        link-aggregation:\n          mode: 802.3ad\n          options:\n            miimon: '100'\n            lacp_rate: 'fast'\n            min_links: '1'\n          port:\n            - <pf_name>\n      - name: ens5f0\n        type: ethernet\n        state: up\n        mtu: 9216"} lang="yaml" title="Example nncpBondF0Worker0.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> specifies the node where the bonded interface is created.</li>
      <li><code>mode: 802.3ad</code> sets the LACP mode to enable LACP on the bond.</li>
      <li><code>lacp_rate: 'fast'</code> sets the LACP rate on the interface. You must also set the rate to <code>fast</code> on the switch. The <code>fast</code> rate sends LACP packets every second.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;pf_name&gt;</code> specifies the PF that you want to include in the bond.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy</code> resource for the <code>ens5f1</code> interface on the <code>worker-0</code> node:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1\nkind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: example-bond-f1\nspec:\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: <node_name>\n  desiredState:\n    interfaces:\n      - name: example-bond-f1\n        description: example-bond-f1\n        type: bond\n        state: up\n        mtu: 9216\n        link-aggregation:\n          mode: 802.3ad\n          options:\n            miimon: '100'\n            lacp_rate: 'fast'\n            min_links: '1'\n          port:\n            - <pf_name>\n      - name: ens5f1\n        type: ethernet\n        state: up\n        mtu: 9216"} lang="yaml" title="Example nncpBondF1Worker0.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> specifies the node where the bonded interface is created.</li>
      <li><code>mode: 802.3ad</code> sets the LACP mode to enable LACP on the bond.</li>
      <li><code>lacp_rate: 'fast'</code> sets the LACP rate on the interface. You must also set the rate to <code>fast</code> on the switch. The <code>fast</code> rate sends LACP packets every second.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;pf_name&gt;</code> specifies the PF that you want to include in the bond.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the resources by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f nncpBondF0Worker0.yaml\n$ oc apply -f nncpBondF1Worker0.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create SR-IOV network VFs for the bonded interfaces:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>SriovNetworkNodePolicy</code> resource for the <code>ens5f0</code> interface on the <code>worker-0</code> node:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetworkNodePolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetpolicy-port-0\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  deviceType: netdevice\n  nicSelector:\n    pfNames:\n      - <pf_name>\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: <node_name>\n  numVfs: <num_vfs>\n  priority: 99\n  resourceName: <resource_name>"} lang="yaml" title="Example sriovnetworkpolicy-port1.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;pf_name&gt;</code> specifies the PF to create the VFs from.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> specifies the node where the VFs are created.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;num_vfs&gt;</code> specifies the number of VFs to create on the PF.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;resource_name&gt;</code> specifies the resource name used by pods to request these VFs.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>SriovNetworkNodePolicy</code> resource for the <code>ens5f1</code> interface on the <code>worker-0</code> node:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetworkNodePolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetpolicy-port-1\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  deviceType: netdevice\n  nicSelector:\n    pfNames:\n      - <pf_name>\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: <node_name>\n  numVfs: <num_vfs>\n  priority: 99\n  resourceName: <resource_name>"} lang="yaml" title="Example sriovnetworkpolicy-port2.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;pf_name&gt;</code> specifies the PF to create the VFs from.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> specifies the node where the VFs are created.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;num_vfs&gt;</code> specifies the number of VFs to create on the PF.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;resource_name&gt;</code> specifies the resource name used by pods to request these VFs.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the resources by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f sriovnetworkpolicy-port1.yaml\n$ oc apply -f sriovnetworkpolicy-port2.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Configure the PF Status Relay Operator:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>PFLACPMonitor</code> resource. This example file configures the Operator to monitor the LACP status of <code>ens5f0</code> and <code>ens5f1</code> bonded interfaces on the <code>worker-0</code> node:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: pfstatusrelay.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: PFLACPMonitor\nmetadata:\n  namespace: openshift-pf-status-relay-operator\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: pf-status-relay-operator\n  name: pflacpmonitor-worker-0\nspec:\n  interfaces:\n    - <pf_name>\n    - <pf_name>\n  pollingInterval: <polling_interval>\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: <node_name>"} lang="yaml" title="Example pflacpmonitor.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;pf_name&gt;</code> specifies the physical functions to monitor.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;polling_interval&gt;</code> specifies the polling interval in milliseconds to check the LACP status on the monitored interfaces. The minimum value is <code>1000</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> specifies the node for the target interfaces.</li>
      </ul>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Use only one <code>PFLACPMonitor</code> custom resource to monitor each network interface on a node. If you create multiple resources that target the same interface, the PF Status Relay Operator will not process the conflicting configurations.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>PFLACPMonitor</code> resource by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f pflacpmonitor.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check the logs of the PF Status Relay Operator to verify that it is monitoring the LACP state:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs -n openshift-pf-status-relay-operator <pf_status_relay_operator_pod_name>"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"{\"time\":\"2025-07-24T13:35:54.653201692Z\",\"level\":\"INFO\",\"msg\":\"lacp is up\",\"interface\":\"ens5f0\"}\n{\"time\":\"2025-07-24T13:35:54.65347273Z\",\"level\":\"INFO\",\"msg\":\"vf link state was set\",\"id\":0,\"state\":\"auto\",\"interface\":\"ens5f0\"}\n..."} lang="bash" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>SriovNetwork</code> resources to make the VFs available for use within the <code>sriov-operator-tests</code> namespace:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>SriovNetwork</code> resource for the VFs created on <code>ens5f0</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetwork\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetwork-port0\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  capabilities: '{ \"mac\": true }'\n  networkNamespace: sriov-operator-tests\n  resourceName: resourceport0"} lang="yaml" title="Example sriovnetwork-port1.yaml file" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>SriovNetwork</code> resource for the VFs created on <code>ens5f1</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetwork\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetwork-port1\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  capabilities: '{ \"mac\": true }'\n  networkNamespace: sriov-operator-tests\n  resourceName: resourceport1"} lang="yaml" title="Example sriovnetwork-port2.yaml file" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the resources by running the following commands:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f sriovnetwork-port1.yaml\n$ oc apply -f sriovnetwork-port2.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Define a high-availability pod that uses the SR-IOV VFs:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>NetworkAttachmentDefinition</code> resource to create an <code>active-backup</code> bond using the two SR-IOV networks:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1\nkind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition\nmetadata:\n  name: nad-bond-1\n  namespace: sriov-operator-tests\nspec:\n  config: |-\n    {\"type\": \"bond\", \"cniVersion\": \"0.3.1\", \"name\": \"bond-net1\",\n    \"mode\": \"active-backup\", \"failOverMac\": 1, \"linksInContainer\": true, \"miimon\": \"100\", \"mtu\": 1450,\n    \"links\": [{\"name\": \"net1\"},{\"name\": \"net2\"}], \"capabilities\": {\"ips\": true}, \"ipam\": {\"type\": \"static\"}}"} lang="yaml" title="Example nad-bond.yaml file" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>linksInContainer: true</code> creates the bond inside the pod’s network namespace.</li>
      <li><code>mode: active-backup</code> configures the bond to use active-backup mode.</li>
      <li>
      <code>links</code> specifies the pod-level interfaces to include in the bond.
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The PF Status Relay Operator provides LACP state monitoring for pod-level bonding with the <code>mode: active-backup</code> configuration only.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>NetworkAttachmentDefinition</code> resource by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f nad-bond.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a YAML file that defines the <code>Pod</code> resource that uses the VFs from the bonded interfaces in active-backup mode:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: client-bond\n  namespace: sriov-operator-tests\n  annotations:\n    k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: |-\n      [{\n          \"name\": \"sriovnetwork-port0\",\n          \"interface\": \"net1\",\n          \"mac\": \"<mac_address>\"\n        },{\n          \"name\": \"sriovnetwork-port1\",\n          \"interface\": \"net2\",\n          \"mac\": \"<mac_address>\"\n        },{\n          \"name\": \"nad-bond-1\",\n          \"interface\": \"bond0\",\n          \"ips\": [\"192.168.10.254/24\",\"2001:100::254/64\"],\n          \"mac\": \"<mac_address>\"\n      }]\nspec:\n  nodeName: worker-0\n  containers:\n    - name: client-bond\n      image: quay.io/nginx/nginx-unprivileged\n      imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent\n      command: [\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", \"sleep 3650d\"]\n      securityContext:\n        privileged: true\n      command: [\"/bin/sleep\", \"3650d\"]"} lang="yaml" title="Example client-bond.yaml file" />
      <ul><li>The <code>k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks</code> annotation requests three networks: two SR-IOV VFs, <code>net1</code> and <code>net2</code>, and one bond, <code>bond0</code>, which uses them.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>Pod</code> resource by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f client-bond.yaml"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that the failover mechanism:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Log in to the <code>client-bond</code> pod by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n sriov-operator-tests client-bond"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the initial status of the pod-level bond by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.4# cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"[root@client-bond-tlb /]# cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0 \n...\n\nBonding Mode: transmit load balancing\nTransmit Hash Policy: layer2 (0)\nPrimary Slave: None\nCurrently Active Slave: net1\nMII Status: up\nMII Polling Interval (ms): 100\nUp Delay (ms): 0\nDown Delay (ms): 0\nPeer Notification Delay (ms): 0\n\nSlave Interface: net1\nMII Status: up\nSpeed: 25000 Mbps\nDuplex: full\nLink Failure Count: 0\nPermanent HW addr: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\nSlave queue ID: 0\n\nSlave Interface: net2\nMII Status: up\nSpeed: 25000 Mbps\nDuplex: full\nLink Failure Count: 0\nPermanent HW addr: BB:CC:DD:EE:FF:GG"} lang="bash" title="Example output" />
      <ul><li>Both <code>net1</code> and <code>net2</code> interfaces are up.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>Exit the pod shell.</li>
      <li>Simulate an LACP failure on your upstream physical switch. To simulate this scenario, you can filter LACP traffic on the switch port that you want to test the failure on. This ensures that the physical link remains up while the LACP polling fails. The command to do this is vendor-dependent.</li>
      <li>
      Verify the failover inside the pod by logging back into the <code>client-bond</code> pod and checking the bond status again:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.4# cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"...\n\nBonding Mode: transmit load balancing\nTransmit Hash Policy: layer2 (0)\nPrimary Slave: None\nCurrently Active Slave: net2\nMII Status: up\nMII Polling Interval (ms): 100\nUp Delay (ms): 0\nDown Delay (ms): 0\nPeer Notification Delay (ms): 0\n\nSlave Interface: net1\nMII Status: down\nSpeed: Unknown\nDuplex: Unknown\nLink Failure Count: 1\nPermanent HW addr: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\nSlave queue ID: 0\n\nSlave Interface: net2\nMII Status: up\nSpeed: 25000 Mbps\nDuplex: full\nLink Failure Count: 0\nPermanent HW addr: BB:CC:DD:EE:FF:GG\nSlave queue ID: 0"} lang="bash" title="Example output" />
      <ul>
      <li>
      The <code>net1</code> interface is down, and the <code>net2</code> interface is now the active interface.
      <p>The client-bond pod detects the link state change and switches to the backup network path.</p>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
